### What changed to make REQ-101/102 green

- `skills/economy/gig/lib/lock.mjs`: extracted `isLockStale(nowMs, mtimeMs, staleMs) -> boolean` as an
  independently exported pure function (was inlined in `acquire()`); rewired `acquire()` to call it.
  Additionally fixed a genuine reclaim race the Phase 2a RED evidence flagged as a "noteworthy" open
  finding: the OLD stale-reclaim branch did `fs.unlink(file)` then `fs.open(file, "wx")`, and `unlink` is
  not scoped to "the file I just observed" — a second racer's `unlink` could delete the FIRST racer's
  freshly-recreated lock file, letting both racers' `fs.open("wx")` calls each succeed in turn. FIX:
  reclaim now goes through `fs.rename(file, <unique trash path>)` — POSIX `rename()` requires its SOURCE
  path to exist and is atomic w.r.t. concurrent renames of the same source, so exactly ONE of two
  concurrent reclaimers' renames can succeed; the other's source lookup fails with `ENOENT`. Verified by
  the Tier-2 atomicity test (`★PROP-101c (Tier 2, atomicity)★`), which now passes.
- `skills/economy/gig/gig.mjs`: `applyAndSave()` now retries its OWN acquisition of the shared `"_board"`
  lock with a short, bounded backoff (40 attempts × 5ms) SPECIFICALLY for board-lock contention (detected
  by matching the exact `withGigLock` rejection-reason prefix), never for a genuine business-logic
  rejection. This is scoped ONLY to the shared board lock in `applyAndSave` — `withGigLock`'s general
  per-gigId/`"_post"` behavior (in `lib/lock.mjs`) is completely untouched and remains fail-closed/
  non-queueing, which is why REQ-103's fund-safety tests (self-verify rejection, same-gig double-pay)
  still pass unchanged: those tests exercise the PER-GIG lock, not the board lock. This closes the
  reproducible 3-way finding from the RED-phase evidence (`evidence/sprint-1-red-phase.log` §2): two FAST,
  unrelated gigIds' board-lock acquisitions landing in the same instant no longer cause one to be rejected
  outright — the loser now retries and wins on a subsequent attempt once the winner's brief critical
  section (a local read-mutate-write, not the slow network settle) releases.

