## 4. index.mjs wiring (REQ-201's Effectful Shell layer — no dedicated Phase 2a test, per the RED-phase
   evidence's own documented scope note; verified here via the full regression suites below)

`runtime/loop/index.mjs` now: (a) captures each live slot's `risk`/`alwaysAvailable` fields from
`registry.json` at bootstrap; (b) each wake, computes `hasOpenRiskPositionOf('yield')` via the real
`hasOpenRiskPositionOfYield(recentLedger)` (the SAME already-fetched ledger scan that populates
`ctx.positionsSummary` — zero new I/O) and `hasOpenRiskPositionOf('hl_trade')` via the real
`hasOpenRiskPositionOfHlTrade(...)`, wired to a NEW `queryHlTradeOpenPositions()` helper that shells out to
`skills/earn/hl-trade/hl.py account` (the SAME primitive `skills/earn/run.sh` already invokes in
production) — lazy per `hasOpenRiskPositionOfHlTrade`'s own internal threshold check, fail-open on any
subprocess/parse failure; (c) calls the pure `filterCatalog(...)` with these resolved inputs and passes
its result (not the full unfiltered `activeSkillSlots`) into `assembleContext`, which is the single
upstream source `brain.mjs` reads for both `buildSystemPrompt`'s "Available skill slots" listing and
`getToolDefinitions`'s `run_skill.slot` enum — so a broke instance's actual tool menu is narrowed, not
just a display list. A defensive try/catch around the `filterCatalog` call falls back to the full
unfiltered catalog (with a stderr warning) rather than crashing the wake loop on any unexpected error.

