## Phase 2c (Refactor) — 2026-07-07

Scope: `~/anicca/skills/economy/gig/lib/lock.mjs`, `~/anicca/skills/economy/gig/gig.mjs`,
`~/anicca/runtime/loop/catalog-gate.mjs`, `~/anicca/runtime/loop/index.mjs` (the four files touched by
Phase 2b). No spec change, no new feature, no test change — behavior preserved byte-for-byte per the
externally-observable contract (all Phase 2b test files, unmodified, re-run below).

**Change made:** `skills/economy/gig/lib/lock.mjs` — `acquire()`'s stale-lock-reclaim branch duplicated
the exact "open 'wx' -> close -> EEXIST?" primitive that also appears in `acquire()`'s fast path (same
open/close/catch-EEXIST block, written out twice). Extracted the duplicated block into
`tryCreateLockFile(file)` (returns `true`/`false`/rethrows non-EEXIST, identical semantics to the two
inlined copies) and extracted the reclaim branch itself into `reclaimStaleLock(file, staleMs)`, which
calls `tryCreateLockFile` for both its own reclaim-open attempt and (via `acquire()`) the original fast
path. `acquire()` is now a 4-line dispatcher: `mkdir` -> `tryCreateLockFile` -> (on failure)
`reclaimStaleLock`. No control-flow branch, error code, or return value changed — every `if (e.code !==
"EEXIST") throw e` / `ENOENT` / stale-boundary check is preserved verbatim, just relocated.

**No change made (evaluated, decided against):**
- `gig.mjs`'s `applyAndSave()` bounded-backoff retry (`BOARD_LOCK_RETRY_ATTEMPTS`/`_DELAY_MS`) is already
  a single, clearly named, non-duplicated block. `lib/escrow.mjs` has an unrelated retry loop (variable
  per-attempt delay array, retries on specific transient HTTP verify-reason codes) in the same package —
  structurally different enough (different domain: network verify-lag vs local fs-lock contention;
  different shape: delay array vs fixed attempts+delay) that forcing a shared abstraction would be
  speculative generalization not required by any test (anti-slop). Left both as-is.
- `catalog-gate.mjs`'s `hasOpenRiskPositionOfYield` (sync ledger scan) and `hasOpenRiskPositionOfHlTrade`
  (async, lazy, fail-open network query) look superficially similar (same naming prefix) but have
  materially different contracts (sync vs async, fail-closed-by-omission vs fail-open, zero-I/O vs lazy
  I/O) — merging them would obscure the fail-open/fail-closed asymmetry that PROP-201i's tests exist to
  pin down. Left as two separate functions.
- `runtime/loop/index.mjs`'s wiring (`riskTagOf`/`alwaysAvailableOf`/`hasOpenRiskPositionOf` dispatcher,
  `queryHlTradeOpenPositions`) and `filterCatalog`'s argument-object field names (`balanceUsdc`,
